# 1020. Number of Enclaves

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-enclaves/
- **Topic:** Graph, Matrix, DFS
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

Initially, I thought of counting all the land cells in the grid.

Instead of finding the land cells that cannot escape, I realized it would be easier to find all the land cells connected to the boundary.

Any land cell connected to a boundary land cell can eventually walk outside the grid.

Therefore:

Answer = Total Land Cells - Boundary Connected Land Cells.

## 2. My Approach

1. Count the total number of land cells (1) in the grid.
2. Traverse all four boundaries of the grid.
3. Whenever a boundary cell contains 1, start DFS from that cell.
4. During DFS:
   - Check whether the cell is within the grid boundaries.
   - If the cell is not land or is already visited, return.
   - Mark the visited land cell as -1.
   - Decrement the total land count.
   - Explore all four directions.
5. Return the remaining land count.

I used -1 as a visited marker in the original matrix, avoiding an additional visited matrix.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

Initially, I thought of using a separate Boolean visited matrix.

Later, I realized that modifying the original grid using -1 would eliminate the need for an additional matrix.

I also considered returning the number of connected land cells from DFS, but decrementing the count directly during DFS made the implementation simpler.

## 4. What Clicked

Instead of finding all land cells that cannot escape, find all land cells that CAN escape.

Every land cell connected to a boundary land cell can eventually walk outside the grid.

By marking these cells during DFS and decrementing the initial land count, the remaining count directly gives the answer.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

class Solution {
    int m, n;
    int count = 0;

    public int numEnclaves(int[][] grid) {
        m = grid.length;
        n = grid[0].length;

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    count++;
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            dfs(grid, i, 0);
            dfs(grid, i, n - 1);
        }

        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            dfs(grid, 0, j);
            dfs(grid, m - 1, j);
        }

        return count;
    }

    void dfs(int[][] grid, int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || r >= m || c < 0 || c >= n ||
            grid[r][c] != 1) {
            return;
        }

        grid[r][c] = -1;
        count--;

        dfs(grid, r - 1, c);
        dfs(grid, r + 1, c);
        dfs(grid, r, c - 1);
        dfs(grid, r, c + 1);
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(m * n)
- Auxiliary Space Complexity: O(m * n) in the worst case due to the recursive DFS stack.

## 7. Revision Notes

- Start DFS only from boundary land cells.
- Every land cell connected to the boundary can escape.
- Use the original matrix to mark visited cells.
- Mark visited land cells as -1.
- Decrement the count whenever DFS visits a land cell.
- Avoid processing the same cell multiple times.
- Remaining land cells are the enclaves.
